an  to making achieving racial justice which  he did and pushing through the 64 civil  rights act the 1965 voting rights act  which i think were really where the keys  that were handed to the republicans to  pitch to the dark angels of the south  yeah i i i would tend to agree with you  personally though i have no regrets  about trying to stop the killing that he  was unleashing  well thank you for your comments and and  and your book and  it's been especially um interesting  tonight having some somebody else who  was in the middle of all this to hear  what other people  of the same age were were feeling at the  moment but i wanted to make a comment  and then ask a question related to that  in 1968 i was i was i had been a grunt  in the civil rights movement and i was  getting involved in the anti-war  movement  today i'm still sort of a paid organizer  for many national organizations in the  course of the course of my work in the  last 20 or 30 years i've traveled around  the country a lot as you said you've  done and talked to many people and  at the risk of sounding too optimistic  and a hard time um i've i've come to the  conclusion the last 10 years  that busy as it was exciting as it was  in 1967 68 69 even 70 for those of us  who are there  my feeling in talking particularly to  young people on campuses and you know  democratic party activists and even  republican party activists you know in  various parts of the country  um is that  there's a million times more people  working on the issues that we cared  about in 196 in 1968 than there were  then i i think that the thing that i  have felt and i wonder if you've  agreed with this or not is that  in 1968 was real important in so many  ways because the great unifying thing  was being against the war so you felt if  you went to an anti-war  demonstration if you went to any kind of  a meeting the war always came up and  there was this unifying thing where you  could see who your allies were  now that's not so it's sort of true of  the iraq war but it's not as true  but i find when i look at the  environmental movement and the  anti-sweatshop movement boy there's a  million more people especially people in  their 20s and 30s millions more who are  really involved engaged in every way  more than you know more than i i  don't spend a lot of time on college  campuses but i have been on a few of  late  and it's my impression that um  uh  young people college students are being  very underrated but there's a lot of  them who really do care about issues and  are impassioned about these things and  and  prepared to do things about them i think  it's uh  um  i i think they're being very  underestimated yeah and i think given  what some people have said about you  know what's happened and  being under the radar screen i think  these are the the kids who are under the  radar screen now who are doing really  major work on you know the globalization  issues sweatshop issues all these kinds  of things big big time environmental  stuff and that there may be some sort of  a  major positive explosion in the coming  years of these people i i agree with you  i just want to sneak this last guy in  because i'm over my limit and he's the  last guy and i want to hear what he has  to say  well you may not want to actually  oh no no you could we rerun the tape if  i don't like it  just a footnote  um i was a so in a life a time of  realism when you have only two choices  to make the question is  which was what what were we better off  with so i just want to leave that small  note  that despite the  the sense of loss of hope of that time  it was a pretty difficult experience to  go through in terms of trying to get  where we wanted to get  i'll leave it at that too  thank you  thank you very much  folks  is author of cod salt and the basque  history of the world his latest 1968 the  year that rock the world is published by  valentine bucks visit randomhouse.com  bb for more information  next week o